//
// `ownership.md (O-Move)` — *"if the return BORROWS a parameter, the return type records it
// (`{Attr(param)}`) and the caller COPIES to obtain its own store"*.  So a local bound from
// `fn keep(s: K) -> K { s }` OWNS its record: a write through the local does NOT reach the
// source.  `(B-View)` is the contrast — a struct-typed PROJECTION really is a view, and a write
// through it DOES reach the container.
//
// This is the measurement `formal/ownership.md` D-own-40 and `heap.md` D-heap-6 rest on, and it
// is here because the rule alone is not enough to settle what those entries needed: they turn on
// whether such a local's assignments MIX ownership for `(O-Witness)`, and reading "the return
// borrows" as "the local views" gets the answer backwards.  The copy is what makes a local whose
// other assignment is a CONSTRUCTION own on BOTH paths — so it does not mix, so it must carry no
// owner witness.  It carries one today, which is what D-own-40 records.
//
// The nullable spelling is measured beside the dense one because `@FR-N-Shape` makes `K?` the
// synthetic struct-enum `__nullable<K>`, a different storage route to the same question, and it
// is the spelling the two register entries actually use.
//
// @falsified-at: none — this guard states a rule that HOLDS on every tree back to the filing; it
//   is a control, not a regression catch, so there is no build it fails on.  Its falsification
//   is the THIRD cell: `t_view` writes through and reads 99 from the container, so a run where
//   all three cells answered "owns" would be a harness that had stopped distinguishing them.
//   That is the cell to corrupt first if this file ever looks vacuous.
// CHANNEL: the VALUE read back from the source after a write through the local, identically on
//   both backends (`(O-NoDiverge)`).  Not a release count and not a leak — this file is about
//   which binding OWNS, and the hook-count consequences of getting it wrong are scored by
//   `a-record-local-reassigned-after-a-literal-build-releases-what-it-displaces.loft`.
// ARMED: no instrument.  Ordinary assertions on both backends.
// HOLDS: all four cells, on every tree.

struct K { x: integer }
struct Box { inner: K }

fn keep(s: K) -> K { s }                      // the return BORROWS its parameter
fn mint(s: K, i: integer) -> K { K { x: s.x + i } }   // the return is FRESH

// (O-Move)'s borrowing-return clause: the caller copies, so the local owns its own record and a
// write through it leaves the source alone.
fn test_a_borrowing_returns_result_is_the_locals_own_store() {
  src = K { x: 7 };
  c: K? = keep(src);
  c.x = 99;
  assert(src.x == 7, "the source is untouched by a write through the local: got {src.x}, want 7");
  assert(c.x == 99, "the local took the write: got {c.x}, want 99");
}

// The same question with the NULLABLE wrapper removed — `@FR-N-Shape` says the `?` is a
// nullability bit over the same storage, so the answer may not depend on it.
fn test_the_dense_spelling_answers_the_same() {
  src = K { x: 7 };
  c: K = keep(src);
  c.x = 99;
  assert(src.x == 7, "dense: the source is untouched: got {src.x}, want 7");
}

// CONTROL — a genuinely MINTING callee.  Always owned, and it must read the same as the
// borrowing one, which is the point: `(O-Move)` makes the two indistinguishable at the caller.
fn test_a_minting_return_is_owned_too() {
  src = K { x: 7 };
  c: K? = mint(src, 0);
  c.x = 99;
  assert(src.x == 7, "a minting return: the source is untouched: got {src.x}, want 7");
}

// CONTROL — and the falsification of the three above.  `(B-View)` makes a struct-typed
// PROJECTION a view, so this write DOES reach the container.  Without this cell a harness that
// had stopped writing anything would pass every assertion above.
fn test_a_projection_is_a_view_and_writes_through() {
  b = Box { inner: K { x: 7 } };
  c: K = b.inner;
  c.x = 99;
  assert(b.inner.x == 99, "a projection writes THROUGH to the container: got {b.inner.x}, want 99");
}
